WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:10.902
In 1874 around 3,500 curious people paid one franc to attend an Impressionist art exhibition in Paris.

2
00:00:10.903 --> 00:00:20.754
At this exhibition an unknown artist named Claude Monet showed his painting Impression, Sunrise to the public for the first time.

3
00:00:20.755 --> 00:00:30.626
Monet's painting was a scene of a harbor at sunrise, with noticeable brushstrokes swirling around the canvas in hazy colors.

4
00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:40.624
Other now-famous artists—Edgar Degas, Pierre-Auguste Renoir, and Berthe Morisot—also showed works at this exhibition.

5
00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:46.955
And how did the public react to their paintings? They laughed!

6
00:00:46.956 --> 00:00:52.993
Since the 1600s the most important art exhibition in France had been the Salon.

7
00:00:52.994 --> 00:01:01.413
Every year artists submitted works to a jury of critics who selected the best paintings to exhibit.

8
00:01:01.414 --> 00:01:05.530
The Salon's decisions could make or break an artist's career.

9
00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:13.377
Acceptance meant publicity and opportunities for more work, but getting accepted was not easy.

10
00:01:13.378 --> 00:01:19.791
The Salon had strict preferences. Paintings had to look realistic.

11
00:01:19.792 --> 00:01:25.522
Religious and historical themes were appreciated.

12
00:01:25.523 --> 00:01:33.156
Landscapes, still lifes, and contemporary scenes were not.

13
00:01:33.157 --> 00:01:42.050
Before the Impressionists most painters focused on the Salon's preferred subjects, capturing them in paintings with dark colors.

14
00:01:42.051 --> 00:01:50.433
The painting techniques used by traditional artists hadn't changed much since the Renaissance over four hundred years earlier. The

15
00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:59.842
world, however, was beginning to change in dramatic ways, which made the late nineteenth century an exciting time for artists. Painters

16
00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:06.837
could buy mass-produced paint in tubes, which was far more convenient than making their own. Japan

17
00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:13.464
began trading with Europe, which introduced new styles of art to the Western world. The

18
00:02:13.465 --> 00:02:21.021
city of Paris had been enlarged and modernized, and some artists were eager to modernize art as well. However,

19
00:02:21.022 --> 00:02:29.847
one modern technology represented a growing challenge for artists: photography. Photographs

20
00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:35.847
had become widespread and could perfectly capture reality. Did

21
00:02:35.848 --> 00:02:39.737
anyone need paintings anymore? The

22
00:02:39.738 --> 00:02:51.105
Impressionists believed they could compete with black-and-white photography by using their own unique tools—light and color. Most

23
00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:54.932
artists painted indoors in a studio. The

24
00:02:54.933 --> 00:03:03.345
Impressionists painted outdoors in the open air, or in the cafes and neighborhoods of the city. With

25
00:03:03.346 --> 00:03:12.238
quick, spontaneous brushstrokes, the Impressionists captured colorful impressions of everyday life as they saw them. Painting

26
00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:21.424
realistically didn't matter to the Impressionists, but it still mattered to the Salon, which rejected the Impressionists' work. In

27
00:03:21.425 --> 00:03:28.814
1863 the Salon rejected so many paintings that it became a public controversy. Public

28
00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:33.678
controversy could be dangerous in France. The

29
00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:39.867
angry public had removed kings, politicians, and an emperor before. Emperor

30
00:03:39.868 --> 00:03:44.721
Napoleon III did not want that to happen to him. He

31
00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:51.314
decided that the rejected works should be displayed to the public in a separate exhibition. It

32
00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:57.186
was called the Salon of the Refused, and it attracted a lot of attention. In

33
00:03:57.187 --> 00:04:03.802
fact, more people attended the Salon of the Refused than the Paris Salon! However,

34
00:04:03.803 --> 00:04:08.813
few people took the rejected paintings seriously. The

35
00:04:08.814 --> 00:04:12.817
Impressionists were not discouraged by the poor reception. Instead

36
00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:19.360
they were delighted—the Salon of the Refused made the public aware of their work! The

37
00:04:19.361 --> 00:04:25.359
following year the Impressionists requested that another Salon of the Refused be held. But

38
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.811
the French government refused their request. Further

39
00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:33.950
requests were also denied for the next decade. Impatient

40
00:04:33.951 --> 00:04:41.910
and determined, the Impressionists decided to hold their own exhibition in 1874. Calling

41
00:04:41.911 --> 00:04:50.043
themselves the Anonymous Society of Artists, the Impressionists displayed 165 works. Monet,

42
00:04:50.044 --> 00:04:56.118
Renoir, Degas, and Morisot all participated. Time

43
00:04:56.119 --> 00:05:00.133
had not changed public opinion though. Upon

44
00:05:00.134 --> 00:05:08.000
seeing Impression, Sunrise , one critic wrote that it looked less finished than sketches for wallpaper.

45
00:05:08.001 --> 00:05:13.043
Meanwhile the Impressionists did not give up on the Paris Salon.

46
00:05:13.044 --> 00:05:19.315
They continued to submit their work, and they continued to be rejected. Between

47
00:05:19.316 --> 00:05:27.445
1874 and 1886, the Impressionists held a total of eight independent exhibitions. By

48
00:05:27.446 --> 00:05:34.370
their third in 1877, they were officially calling themselves Impressionists. This

49
00:05:34.371 --> 00:05:45.097
show included Renoir's Ball at the Galette Windmill , a now-famous painting that sold for $78 million in 1990.

50
00:05:45.098 --> 00:05:52.803
With each exhibition different artists displayed works, and something began to change: public opinion.

51
00:05:52.804 --> 00:05:58.032
People were gradually opening their eyes to the beauty of Impressionist art.

52
00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:06.938
Even the Salon began accepting the Impressionists' work, and artists like Monet finally achieved mainstream success.

53
00:06:06.939 --> 00:06:14.068
Impressionism itself evolved as newer artists experimented with new techniques.

54
00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:23.455
Small, delicate brushstrokes were replaced with bold shapes by Paul Cezanne and Paul Gauguin.

55
00:06:23.456 --> 00:06:30.298
Georges-Pierre Seurat painted scenes made of tiny dots of paint.

56
00:06:30.299 --> 00:06:35.361
These painters eventually became known as Post-Impressionists.

57
00:06:35.362 --> 00:06:43.356
Later artists like Vincent van Gogh continued to experiment and develop their own unique styles.

58
00:06:43.357 --> 00:06:52.855
Today the Impressionists are remembered as genius artists, respected as deeply as the realistic artists they rebelled against.

59
00:06:52.856 --> 00:06:57.235
Their paintings sell for millions of dollars.

60
00:06:57.236 --> 00:07:04.719
Just like in 1874, the Impressionists can draw large crowds to museums and galleries.

61
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:13.883
But you won't hear anyone laughing at them anymore!

